The quantum null energy condition (QNEC) establishes a lower limit on the energy-momentum tensor, relating it to how the entanglement entropy of a sub-region varies along a null direction. Investigating the quantum thermodynamics of many-body systems within two-dimensional holographic theories, the authors examine whether the QNEC constrains irreversible entropy production during dynamics. They determine analytical lower bounds for the entropy change corresponding various processes consistent with recent findings. The study reveals that while the QNEC is relevant, it is insufficient on its own; consequently, an updated, stronger version of the QNEC is proposed and verified against precise computations in the AdS/CFA. This validated framework includes one-dimensional quantum systems both within and beyond the holographic dual of the AdS/C. The proposed bound relates the variation of entropy change along a null direction to the local divergence of entropy production and the energy momentum tensor, expressed as:
